body_text:
When an extension is compiled for `abi3t`, the array accessor functions like `PyArray_DATA`, `PyArray_NDIM`, `PyArray_FLAGS`, and `PyArray_DESCR` each call `_PyArray_GET_ITEM_DATA`. In abi3 and version-specific builds they compile to an inline cast.

In a real extension (tokenizers, through rust-numpy), a `decode()` call that takes a small uint32 ndarray makes 20 of these calls and runs about 15% slower under abi3t than under abi3. See https://github.com/huggingface/tokenizers/issues/2488#issuecomment-5970712841.

### Reproducer

`fields.c`:

```c
#include <Python.h>
#include <numpy/arrayobject.h>

/* Reads a few ndarray fields per call, like a typical extension function
   that validates its array argument and then borrows its buffer. */
static PyObject *nbytes(PyObject *m, PyObject *arg) {
    if (!PyArray_Check(arg)) {
        PyErr_SetString(PyExc_TypeError, "expected ndarray");
        return NULL;
    }
    PyArrayObject *arr = (PyArrayObject *)arg;
    if (!PyArray_ISCARRAY_RO(arr) || PyArray_DATA(arr) == NULL) {
        PyErr_SetString(PyExc_ValueError, "expected C-contiguous data");
        return NULL;
    }
    npy_intp n = PyArray_ITEMSIZE(arr);
    for (int i = 0; i < PyArray_NDIM(arr); i++)
        n *= PyArray_DIM(arr, i);
    return PyLong_FromSsize_t(n);
}

static int exec(PyObject *m) { return PyArray_ImportNumPyAPI(); }

static PyMethodDef methods[] = {{"nbytes", nbytes, METH_O, NULL}, {NULL}};
PyABIInfo_VAR(abi_info);
static PySlot slots[] = {
    PySlot_DATA(Py_mod_abi, &abi_info),
    PySlot_DATA(Py_mod_name, "fields"),
    PySlot_STATIC_DATA(Py_mod_methods, methods),
    PySlot_FUNC(Py_mod_exec, exec),
    PySlot_DATA(Py_mod_gil, Py_MOD_GIL_NOT_USED),
    PySlot_END,
};
PyMODEXPORT_FUNC PyModExport_fields(void) { return slots; }
```

`bench.py`:

```python
import sys, timeit
import numpy as np
import fields

a = np.zeros(1000, dtype=np.uint32)
assert fields.nbytes(a) == a.nbytes
t = min(timeit.repeat(lambda: fields.nbytes(a), number=1_000_000, repeat=9)) / 1_000_000
print(f"{sys.version.split()[0]} numpy {np.__version__} {fields.__file__.rsplit('/', 1)[-1]}: {t * 1e9:.1f} ns/call")
```

Build one copy for abi3 and one for abi3t, then run both on the same GIL-enabled interpreter:

```sh
NP_INC=$(python3.15 -c "import numpy; print(numpy.get_include())")
FLAGS="-O2 -shared -fPIC -DNPY_NO_DEPRECATED_API=NPY_2_5_API_VERSION -DNPY_TARGET_VERSION=NPY_2_5_API_VERSION -I$NP_INC"
mkdir abi3 abi3t
gcc $FLAGS -DPy_LIMITED_API=0x030f0000 -I"$(python3.15 -c 'import sysconfig; print(sysconfig.get_path("include"))')" fields.c -o abi3/fields.abi3.so
gcc $FLAGS -DPy_TARGET_ABI3T=0x030f0000 -I"$(python3.15t -c 'import sysconfig; print(sysconfig.get_path("include"))')" fields.c -o abi3t/fields.abi3t.so
PYTHONPATH=abi3 python3.15 bench.py
PYTHONPATH=abi3t python3.15 bench.py
```

### Results

CPython 3.15.0rc2 (GIL-enabled), NumPy 2.5.3, x86-64 Linux, gcc -O2, three alternating runs:

```
3.15.0rc2 numpy 2.5.3 fields.abi3.so: 38.9 ns/call
3.15.0rc2 numpy 2.5.3 fields.abi3t.so: 43.4 ns/call
3.15.0rc2 numpy 2.5.3 fields.abi3.so: 37.8 ns/call
3.15.0rc2 numpy 2.5.3 fields.abi3t.so: 45.9 ns/call
3.15.0rc2 numpy 2.5.3 fields.abi3.so: 38.4 ns/call
3.15.0rc2 numpy 2.5.3 fields.abi3t.so: 45.4 ns/call
```

About 25 ns of each call is Python call overhead that both builds share.

## AI disclosure

I used an AI model to create the reproducer C example above.

