body_text:
The design of `moved` and `removed` is intended to support as best as possible cooperation between separate modules maintained by different people which might nonetheless all contribute metadata about how a remote object's modeling as OpenTofu resource instances has changed over time.

One specific example of this is that one module might contain a `moved` block whose `to` address refers (directly or indirectly) to a resource instance address that is no longer desired but has a `removed` block. Here's a simple example of that within a single module, but this is also possible (and more likely to happen) across module boundaries:

```hcl
moved {
  from = aws_instance.a
  to   = aws_instance.b
}

removed {
  from = aws_instance.b
  lifecycle {
    destroy = false
  }
}
```

In this case, if the previous round state has an object bound to `aws_instance.a` then we must plan to destroy that object _as if_ it were bound to `aws_instance.b`, so that it would be proposed for "forget" instead of "delete".

At the time of opening this issue it isn't known yet whether this is working correctly or not. This issue could potentially be closed just by demonstrating that there is existing test coverage for this situation and that those tests are passing against the new runtime implementation.

