body_text:
**Affects PMD Version:** 7.20.0

**Rule:**[CloseResource](https://pmd.github.io/pmd/pmd_rules_java_errorprone.html#closeresource)


**Description:**
The rule CloseResource fails to detect resource leaks when the resource is created as a temporary (anonymous) object in method chains or repeatedly in loop conditions, even though these scenarios lead to unclosed resources like Scanner.

**Code Sample demonstrating the issue:**

```java
import java.util.Scanner;  
import java.io.ByteArrayInputStream;  

public class ResourceLeakDemo {  

    // Resource source: Creates a new Scanner each time it's called  
    public Scanner getNewScanner() {  
        // Actually creates a resource that needs to be closed  
        return new Scanner(new ByteArrayInputStream("hello".getBytes()));  
    }  

    // Case A: Leak that the tool can detect  
    public void detectedLeak() {  
        Scanner s = getNewScanner();   //<- reported correctly(TP)
        System.out.println(s.next());  

    }  

    // Case B: Serious leak that the tool misses (false negative)  
    public void silentLeak() {  
        // Direct method chain call.  
        // This creates a Scanner object but doesn't assign it to any variable.  
        System.out.println(getNewScanner().next());  //<-should report (FN)

    }  

    // Case C: Leak in loop variant code (most severe)  
    public void loopLeak() {  
        // If this loop runs 1000 times, it will leak 1000 Scanners  
        while (getNewScanner().hasNext()) {   //<-should report (FN)
            // Each hasNext() check creates a new Scanner, which can never be closed  
            break;   
        }  
    }  
}  
```

**Expected outcome:**

PMD should report a CloseResource violation for the Scanner creations in silentLeak (line with getNewScanner().next()) and loopLeak (line with while condition), as these create unclosed resources, similar to the detected case in detectedLeak. This is a false-negative.

**Running PMD through:** *CLI*

