body_text:
**Proposed Rule Name:** `LooseCoupling`

**Proposed Category:** Best Practices

**Description:**
Port of the existing [pmd-java `LooseCoupling`](https://docs.pmd-code.org/latest/pmd_rules_java_bestpractices.html#loosecoupling)
rule to Kotlin. Flags declared types (fields, properties, parameters, return types, local
variables) that use a concrete collection/map implementation (`ArrayList`, `HashMap`,
`LinkedHashMap`, `HashSet`, `LinkedList`, `Hashtable`, `Vector`, ...) instead of the corresponding
interface (`List`, `Map`, `Set`, ...). Excessive coupling to implementation types limits the
ability to swap implementations later.

This rule also serves as a real-world demonstration of the new Kotlin XPath type functions and
attributes added in #6893 — specifically `@TypeName` / `pmd-kotlin:typeIs()` /
`pmd-kotlin:typeIsExactly()` for detecting concrete collection types.

**Kotlin-specific nuance vs. the Java rule:** in Java, `List`/`Map`/`Set` are inherently mutable
interfaces, so there's only one target type to suggest. In Kotlin, `List`/`Map`/`Set` are
**read-only** views, while `MutableList`/`MutableMap`/`MutableSet` are the mutable counterparts.
A naive port that always suggests the read-only interface would be wrong (and often a compile
error at the call site) wherever the concrete type is actually mutated (`put`, `remove`, `add`,
...) after declaration/return. The rule needs to suggest the *narrowest correct* interface based
on how the value is used: read-only access → `Map`/`List`/`Set`; mutating access anywhere →
`MutableMap`/`MutableList`/`MutableSet`.

Verified against a real, unrelated open-source Kotlin Android app (525 `.kt` files): found 5
genuine instances of the pattern, e.g. (anonymized):

**Code Sample:**

```kotlin
class Example {
    // violation - concrete type used for a property
    internal val subscriptions: ArrayList<Closeable> = ArrayList()

    // violation - concrete type used for a var
    private var members: ArrayList<Int> = arrayListOf()

    // violation - concrete return type, but only ever read downstream
    // (order is preserved by the runtime object regardless of the declared type,
    // so loosening to Map is safe here - Kotlin's Map iteration order comes from
    // the underlying object, not the declared interface)
    private fun sortItems(items: Collection<Item>): LinkedHashMap<String, Item> {
        // ...
    }

    // preferred approach
    private val subscriptions: MutableList<Closeable> = mutableListOf()
    private fun sortItems(items: Collection<Item>): Map<String, Item> {
        // ...
    }
}
```

**Possible Properties:**

* `allowedTypes` (string list, default `java.util.Properties`) — mirrors the Java rule's property,
  allowing user-defined types that should be treated like interfaces to be exempted.

**Not in scope / possible follow-up:**
* No visibility restriction (matches upstream Java rule, which flags regardless of
  public/private/internal).
* Choosing between `Map`/`MutableMap` (etc.) suggestions based on actual mutation usage may
  require follow-up refinement; first version may start with a simpler heuristic or a single
  generic message.

**Related:**
