body_text:
**Affects PMD Version:** 7.17.0 to 7.28.0

**Rule:** UnnecessaryBoxing
<https://docs.pmd-code.org/latest/pmd_rules_java_codestyle.html#unnecessaryboxing>

**Description:**

#5971 (7.17.0, the fix for #5948) added an overload check to this rule. Its last clause suppresses the warning for any overload with a reference type at the argument position:

https://github.com/pmd/pmd/blob/5c9271c586d69d4386f14a2f997f9d4ff56a0a75/pmd-java/src/main/java/net/sourceforge/pmd/lang/java/rule/codestyle/UnnecessaryBoxingRule.java#L255-L264

`currentParamType` is always primitive at that point (early return at L243), so the clause does not check whether the boxed argument can be passed to that overload. In the sample, `call(String)` hides the warning, although `call(value)` compiles and still calls `call(int)` (JLS 15.12.2.3).

PMD 7.16.0 reports line 6. 7.17.0 and 7.28.0 don't.

**Code Sample demonstrating the issue:**

```java
class Example {
    static void call(int value) { }
    static void call(String value) { }

    void test(Integer value) {
        call(value.intValue());
    }
}
```

JDK calls are affected the same way, e.g. `new StringBuilder(n.intValue())` because of `StringBuilder(String)`, and `new ArrayList<>(n.intValue())` because of `ArrayList(Collection)`.

**Expected outcome:**

PMD should report a violation at line 6, but doesn't. This is a false-negative.

**Running PMD through:** *Other* (PmdAnalysis API)

