**Affects PMD Version:** 7.28.0 (also 7.29.0-SNAPSHOT)

**Description:**

`TypeOps.isAccessible` accepts a protected member of another package when the class passed as access site is a subclass of the declaring class:

https://github.com/pmd/pmd/blob/5c9271c586d69d4386f14a2f997f9d4ff56a0a75/pmd-java/src/main/java/net/sourceforge/pmd/lang/java/types/TypeOps.java#L2106-L2122

For a qualified method call and for `new C(...)` without a class body, the access site is the innermost enclosing class of the call (MethodInvocMirror.java:72, CtorInvocMirror.java:103).

JLS 6.6.2 permits the access in the body of a subclass S, and that body includes the classes declared in S. From `Sub.Inner` in the sample, `sub.m(value)` does not see `protected m(Object)` and resolves to `m(int)`. javac calls `m(Object)` through the synthetic accessor `Sub.access$000`.

A protected instance method may be accessed through a qualifier only if the type of the qualifier is S or a subclass of S (JLS 6.6.2.1). `base.m(value)` with a `Base base` resolves to `m(Object)` in PMD. javac selects `m(int)`. For `base.m((Object) value)` it reports `no suitable method found for m(Object)` with the reason `m(Object) has protected access in Base`.

A protected constructor is accessible to `super(...)` and to an anonymous class, but not to `new C(...)` outside its package (JLS 6.6.2.2). `new Base(value)` resolves to `Base(Object)` in PMD. javac selects `Base(int)`.

Method references go through the same filter (ExprOps.java:506 and :537). `Base::m` resolves to `m(Object)` and `Base::new` to `Base(Object)` in PMD, where javac binds the `int` overloads.

Found while working on #7136.

**Code Sample demonstrating the issue:**

```java
package p;

public class Base {
    public Base(int value) { }
    protected Base(Object value) { }
    public void m(int value) { }
    protected void m(Object value) { }
}
```

```java
package q;

import p.Base;

public class Sub extends Base {
    Sub() { super(0); }

    void test(Base base, Integer value) {
        base.m(value);   // javac: m(int)      PMD: m(Object)
        new Base(value); // javac: Base(int)   PMD: Base(Object)
    }

    class Inner {
        void test(Sub sub, Integer value) {
            sub.m(value); // javac: m(Object)  PMD: m(int)
        }
    }
}
```

**Steps to reproduce:**

1. `javac -d classes p/Base.java q/Sub.java`, also with `--release 8`, then `javap -c -p -cp classes q.Sub 'q.Sub$Inner'`: `Sub.test` calls `p/Base.m:(I)V` and `p/Base."<init>":(I)V`, `Inner.test` calls `q/Sub.access$000`, which invokes `m:(Ljava/lang/Object;)V`.
2. Run PMD on `q/Sub.java` with `classes` on the auxiliary classpath and the XPath rules `//MethodCall[pmd-java:matchesSig("p.Base#m(java.lang.Object)")]` and `//ConstructorCall[pmd-java:matchesSig("p.Base#new(java.lang.Object)")]`. They report lines 9 and 10, and not line 15.

**Running PMD through:** *Other* (PmdAnalysis API)

