body_text:
**Affects PMD Version:** 7.28.0 (also 7.29.0-SNAPSHOT)

**Description:**

In the first sample, javac selects `f(int, Function)` and `m(Supplier, int)` in phase 1. PMD finds both overloads of each pair applicable in the loose phase only and ends with an ambiguity, so `getOverloadSelectionInfo().isFailed()` is true for both calls.

In the strict phase, the result of a lambda body and the return type of a method reference are checked with the checker of that phase, which does not box:

https://github.com/pmd/pmd/blob/5c9271c586d69d4386f14a2f997f9d4ff56a0a75/pmd-java/src/main/java/net/sourceforge/pmd/lang/java/types/internal/infer/Infer.java#L948-L981

`ExprCheckHelper` uses that checker for method reference return types (lines 374 and 480) and for lambda result expressions (line 635). So `() -> 1` is rejected for `Supplier<Integer>`, and `LambdaResult::len` with `int len(String)` for `Function<String, Integer>`. JLS 15.27.3 requires each result expression to be "compatible with R in an assignment context", and a note there says that "the result expression appears in an assignment context, regardless of the context enclosing the lambda expression". 15.13.2 checks the return type of a method reference in an assignment context too. Boxing is allowed there (JLS 5.2).

The second sample adds an implicitly typed lambda, so the invocation phase is not skipped. It rebuilds the declaration without the failed flag (Infer.java:387-395), and the AST ends up with `m(Supplier, Integer, Predicate)` as the selected method. javac selects `m(Supplier, int, Predicate)` in phase 1. UnusedPrivateMethod reports that method on line 6 as unused.

Found while working on #7136.

**Code Sample demonstrating the issue:**

```java
import java.util.function.Function;
import java.util.function.Supplier;
import java.util.function.ToIntFunction;

public class LambdaResult {
    static int len(String s) { return s.length(); }

    static void f(int x, Function<String, Integer> g) { }
    static void f(Integer x, ToIntFunction<String> g) { }

    static void m(Supplier<Integer> s, int x) { }
    static void m(Supplier<Integer> s, Integer x) { }

    void t(int i) {
        f(i, LambdaResult::len);
        m(() -> 1, i);
    }
}
```

```java
import java.util.function.Predicate;
import java.util.function.Supplier;

public class ImplicitLambda {
    private static void m(Supplier<Integer> s, Integer x, Predicate<String> p) { }
    private static void m(Supplier<Integer> s, int x, Predicate<String> p) { }

    void t(int i) {
        m(() -> 1, i, t -> t.isEmpty());
    }
}
```

**Steps to reproduce:**

1. `javac LambdaResult.java ImplicitLambda.java`, also with `--release 8`, then `javap -c -p LambdaResult ImplicitLambda`. `LambdaResult` calls `f:(ILjava/util/function/Function;)V` and `m:(Ljava/util/function/Supplier;I)V`. `ImplicitLambda` calls `m:(Ljava/util/function/Supplier;ILjava/util/function/Predicate;)V`.
2. Run PMD on `LambdaResult.java` with any Java rule and `PMD_JAVA_X_TYPE_INFERENCE_LOGGING=simple`. stderr shows `[WARNING] Ambiguity error: all methods are maximally specific` for both calls.
3. Run PMD on `ImplicitLambda.java` with `category/java/bestpractices.xml/UnusedPrivateMethod`. It reports line 6.

**Running PMD through:** *Other* (PmdAnalysis API)

