**Affects PMD Version:** 7.28.0 (also 7.29.0-SNAPSHOT)

**Description:**

In the sample, `Inner` declares `m(long)` and the enclosing class `Comb` declares `m(Object)`. For `m(value)` with an `Integer value`, javac searches only the innermost class that has a method named `m` (JLS 15.12.1), so `Inner.m(long)` is the only candidate and the call compiles to `m:(J)V`. PMD resolves the call to `Comb.m(Object)`.

The unqualified lookup merges the methods of the enclosing class into the scope of the inner class and shadows only override-equivalent ones:

https://github.com/pmd/pmd/blob/5c9271c586d69d4386f14a2f997f9d4ff56a0a75/pmd-java/src/main/java/net/sourceforge/pmd/lang/java/symbols/table/internal/JavaResolvers.java#L291-L329

The merger is installed in `SymTableFactory` (lines 410-412). Both methods then reach the overload resolution, `m(Object)` is applicable by strict invocation and wins.

Found while working on #7136.

**Code Sample demonstrating the issue:**

```java
class Comb {
    void m(Object o) { }

    class Inner {
        void m(long x) { }

        void t(Integer value) {
            m(value);
        }
    }
}
```

**Steps to reproduce:**

1. `javac Comb.java`, also with `--release 8`, then `javap -c -p 'Comb$Inner'`: the call is `m:(J)V`.
2. Run PMD on `Comb.java` with the XPath rule `//MethodCall[pmd-java:matchesSig("Comb#m(java.lang.Object)")]`. It reports line 8.

**Running PMD through:** *Other* (PmdAnalysis API)

