body_text:
**Affects PMD Version:** 7.24.0

**Rule:** UnusedPrivateMethod — https://docs.pmd-code.org/latest/pmd_rules_java_bestpractices.html#unusedprivatemethod

**Description:**

The `UnusedPrivateMethod` rule correctly recognises private factory methods referenced from JUnit 5's `@MethodSource` when the simple form (`@MethodSource("foo")`) is used. However, when the same factory method is referenced through the equivalent fully-qualified form documented by JUnit (`@MethodSource("fqcn#methodName")`), PMD fails to follow the reference and reports the private method as unused.

This is a false positive: per the [JUnit 5 docs for `@MethodSource`](https://junit.org/junit5/docs/current/api/org.junit.jupiter.params/org/junit/jupiter/params/provider/MethodSource.html), the value may be a simple method name or a fully-qualified `fully.qualified.ClassName#methodName` reference, and both resolve to the same factory method.

**Code Sample demonstrating the issue:**

```java
package demo.after;

import java.util.stream.Stream;
import org.junit.jupiter.params.ParameterizedTest;
import org.junit.jupiter.params.provider.Arguments;
import org.junit.jupiter.params.provider.MethodSource;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions;

public class StringUtilsTest {

    // Used as the factory method for testTrimAndUpperCase below.
    private static Stream<Arguments> trimAndUpperCaseArguments() {
        return Stream.of(
                Arguments.of("foo"),
                Arguments.of("bar"),
                Arguments.of("baz")
        );
    }

    // Fully-qualified form — PMD does NOT recognise this as a usage.
    @MethodSource("demo.after.StringUtilsTest#trimAndUpperCaseArguments")
    @ParameterizedTest
    void testTrimAndUpperCase(String input) {
        Assertions.assertNotNull(input);
    }
}
```

PMD output:

```
StringUtilsTest.java:13:	UnusedPrivateMethod:	Avoid unused private methods such as 'trimAndUpperCaseArguments()'.
```

Replacing the annotation with the simple form `@MethodSource("trimAndUpperCaseArguments")` makes the warning disappear, although both forms refer to the same method.

**Expected outcome:** PMD should treat the fully-qualified `Class#method` form in `@MethodSource` the same way as the simple form and not flag the referenced private static method as unused.

**Running PMD through:** CLI

