body_text:
**Affects PMD Version:** 7.24.0

**Rule:** ClassWithOnlyPrivateConstructorsShouldBeFinal — https://docs.pmd-code.org/latest/pmd_rules_java_design.html#classwithonlyprivateconstructorsshouldbefinal

**Description:**

`ClassWithOnlyPrivateConstructorsShouldBeFinal` correctly flags a class whose only constructor is explicitly declared `private`. The same class is silently accepted when the same private no-arg constructor is provided by Lombok via `@NoArgsConstructor(access = AccessLevel.PRIVATE)`, even though after annotation processing the two classes are structurally identical.

This means the warning can be made to disappear by switching from an explicit constructor to the Lombok-annotated form, although both forms produce an equivalent "private constructor only" class and should be made `final`.

**Code Sample demonstrating the issue:**

Lombok form (NOT flagged — false negative):

```java
import lombok.AccessLevel;
import lombok.NoArgsConstructor;

@NoArgsConstructor(access = AccessLevel.PRIVATE)
public class Foo {
    public static Foo get() {
        return null;
    }
}
```

Explicit form (flagged correctly):

```java
public class Foo {
    private Foo() {
    }

    public static Foo get() {
        return null;
    }
}
```

PMD output:

```
after.java:5:	ClassWithOnlyPrivateConstructorsShouldBeFinal:	This class has only private constructors and may be final
```

The Lombok variant is silently passed.

**Expected outcome:** PMD should treat `@NoArgsConstructor(access = AccessLevel.PRIVATE)` (and `@AllArgsConstructor`/`@RequiredArgsConstructor` with `AccessLevel.PRIVATE`) as introducing a private constructor for the purposes of this rule, so both variants are flagged consistently.

**Running PMD through:** CLI

